  "strategy": "The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem seeks a step function on [0,2] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum overlap with its periodic shifts. The proposed approach treats this as maximizing the minimum auto-correlation between function samples, which is equivalent to minimizing the overlap.\n\n**1. Approach and Rationale:**\n- **Central Idea:** The overlap integral is the negative of auto-correlation between periodic shifts. Maximizing the minimum auto-correlation thus minimizes the overlap.\n- **Bottleneck:** Previous methods relied on gradient-free heuristics that could converge slowly. This approach uses gradient-based optimization to systematically improve the solution via auto-correlation analysis.\n- **Algorithm Selection:** Gradient ascent with clipping and normalization to ensure solution stays within the valid range and satisfies the integral constraint. Uses nested vectorization for efficiency.\n\n**2. Mathematical Specification:**\n- **Variables:** 400-dimensional vector \\(h\\) (initialized from parent solution) satisfying \\(0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1\\) and \\(\\sum h[i] = 200\\) (ensuring integral 1 over domain [0,2]).\n- **Objective:** Minimize \\(C_5 = \\max_k \\int h(x)(1-h(x+k)) \\,dx\\)\n- **Equivalence:** \\( \\int h(x)(1-h(x+k)) \\,dx = 1 - \\int h(x)h(x+k) \\,dx \\) (taking periodic extension). Maximizing the minimum of the auto-correlation \\(\\int h(x)h(x+k) \\,dx\\) directly minimizes \\(C_5\\).\n- **Constraints:**\n  - \\(\\sum h[i] = 200\\)\n  - \\(0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1\\)\n- **Derivation:** The gradient ascent updates are derived from:\n  - \\(\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial h[j]} \\int h^2(x) \\,dx = h[(j+k)\\%400] \\) multiplied by the autocorrelation coefficient \\(dx\\).\n\n**3. Implementation Procedure:**\n- **Initialization:** Start with provided parent solution (400 points).\n- **Gradient Ascent:**\n  - **Compute Auto-correlation:** For each lag \\(k\\), compute \\(\\text{autocorr}[k] = dx \\cdot \\sum_j h[j] \\cdot h[(j+k)\\%400]\\).\n  - **Identify Minimum Auto-correlation:** Find the minimum over \\(k\\) and collect the active set of lags achieving this minimum.\n  - **Gradient Calculation:** For each vector index \\(j\\), the gradient component is \\(\\frac{dx}{|\\text{active}_k|} \\sum_{k \\in \\text{active}} h[(j+k)\\%400]\\).\n  - **Update Vector:** Perform \\(h[j] = h[j] + \\text{alpha} \\times \\text{gradient}[j]\\) followed by clipping to [0,1] and normalization to satisfy sum=200.\n- **Termination:** After 1000 iterations or failure to improve \\(\\text{autocorr}_{ \\min}\\), accept best solution and recompute \\(C_5\\).\n\n**4. Search Choices and Variations:**\n- **Fixed Parameters:** \n  - \\(\\text{alpha} = 0.0004\\) (learning rate)\n  - Max iterations = 1000\n  - Tolerance = 0.001 (auto-correlation improvement threshold)\n  - Periodicity enforcement via \\(\\%400\\) indexing.\n- **Key Risks:** \n  - Slow convergence rate requires monitoring.\n  - Suboptimal normalization could trap solutions in constrained spaces.\n\n**5. Computation Budget and Robustness:**\n- **Time Estimate:** Each iteration performs 400 gradient updates (with circular shifts). 1000 iterations is feasible within 1300s budget with NumPy optimization.\n- **Memory:** Stores only current \\(h\\) vector and autocorrelation buffer, total ~800 floats.\n- **Validation:** Ensure \\(0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1\\), \\(\\sum h[i] \\approx 200\\). Finally, verify \\(C_5 = 1 - \\min_k (\\text{autocorr}[k]/dx)\\).\n\n**6. Validation and Return Contract:**\n- **Feasibility:** Check 0-constraints, integral close to 200 (within 1e-5).\n- **Finite Values:** Ensure autocorrelation does not diverge.\n- **Objective Consistency:** Recompute \\(C_5\\) using \\(\\text{max}(\\text{autocorrelation}) \\times dx\\).\n- **Fallback:** If optimization fails, use provided parent solution.",
